Uneven tans are no fun, especially if you’re putting in a lot of effort to make your skin that perfect shade of tan. If you prefer to get tan naturally, there are few extra precautions you can take to keep your skin bronzed instead of burnt.
1.Scrub your skin with an exfoliant a week before you tan.
Moisturizing is a great habit regardless, but it’s especially useful if you’re looking into natural tanning. Apply your go-to moisturizer over legs, arms, and all other skin you plan on tanning naturally.
You can also use a facial sunscreen, which is often formulated with less oils and feel lighter on your face.
The skin on your face tends to be more sensitive while also receiving more sun exposure than the rest of your body. Facial sun damage can not only lead to sunburns, but increased wrinkles, fine lines, and brown spots over time as well.
7.Stop naturally tanning after about 1 hour so you don’t burn.
If you spend too much time in the sun, you may be setting yourself for a nasty sunburn, which can definitely lead to an uneven tan. Too much sunlight can also give your skin UV damage.
Instead, plan to tan in the morning or late afternoon, which will help protect your skin from harsh sunlight. Sunburn won’t do you any favors for your tanning goals, and may make your skin tone look inconsistent, which isn’t ideal.
Bronzer mixed with moisturizer is a good cover-up option if you’re looking for a quick fix.
Hop in the shower, then towel-dry your skin. Grab a bottle of lotion labeled as “after-care,” or something similar and spread this lotion over any skin that was exposed to direct sunlight.
Use your favorite exfoliant before you plan on applying any kind of fake tanning product. The scrub will clear away any dead skin from your legs, arms, and any other place you plan on tanning.
3.Get rid of any hair from the spots you’re planning to self-tan.
You can get a tanning mitt online if your tanning pack doesn’t come with one.
Spread the tanning product over your face.
Don’t slip into skintight clothes while your tanning product dries—this could cause it to smear, or look patchy and streaky. Instead, relax in some oversized sweatpants and a baggy shirt, which gives your skin plenty of breathing room.
