The third cause of engine stall difficulties is improper adjustment of the throttle control mechanism.
When the vehicle throttle control handle is in the position of maximum fuel supply, the outer lever control arm of the governor should collide with the high-speed limit screw, so as to limit the maximum fuel supply and maximum speed. When the throttle control handle is pulled to the flushing position, the outer lever control arm of the governor collides with the low speed limit screw, causing the diesel engine to flutter.
